\section{Weak instruments}{

The confidence intervals in \code{conf.low} and \code{conf.high} are Wald intervals,
and they rely on a strong first stage. When the instruments barely move the
endogenous regressor, they can cover the true coefficient far less often
than \code{1 - alpha}; a small first-stage F from \code{diagnostics = TRUE} is the
usual warning sign.

The Anderson-Rubin (1949) test keeps its size however weak the instruments
are, and \code{\link[=lm_robust]{lm_robust()}} computes it directly. To test that the coefficient
on the endogenous regressor \code{x} equals \code{b}, regress \code{y - b * x} on the
excluded instruments and the exogenous regressors, with the \code{se_type},
\code{clusters}, \code{weights}, and \code{fixed_effects} of the IV fit, and test that the
coefficients on the excluded instruments are zero. With one instrument that
test is the instrument's own row of the fit; with several it is the
\code{joint_hypothesis} of
\code{lh_robust(..., linear_hypothesis = c("z1 = 0", "z2 = 0"))}. At
\code{b = 0} the regression is the reduced form, which in an experiment that
randomizes the instrument is the intent-to-treat regression.

The Anderson-Rubin confidence set is every \code{b} that the test does not
reject, found over a grid (see the examples). Under a weak first stage it
need not be an interval: it can be the union of two rays or the whole real
line, which says that the data rule out no value of the coefficient. A set
that reaches either end of the grid should be checked on a wider one. With
several endogenous regressors, the same regression with \code{y} minus each
regressor times its hypothesized value tests all of them jointly.
}

\examples{
set.seed(25)
n <- 200
dat <- data.frame(z = rbinom(n, 1, 0.5), cl = rep(1:20, each = 10))
dat$x <- dat$z * rbinom(n, 1, 0.7)
dat$y <- dat$x + rnorm(n)

# Endogenous regressor on the left of the bar, instrument on the right
fit <- iv_robust(y ~ x | z, data = dat)
tidy(fit)

# Exogenous regressors, endogenous regressors, and instruments, each once
dat$w <- rnorm(n)
iv_robust(y ~ w | x | z, data = dat)

# The same variance menu as lm_robust()
iv_robust(y ~ x | z, data = dat, se_type = "classical")
iv_robust(y ~ x | z, data = dat, clusters = cl)

# The first stage, one lm_robust fit per endogenous regressor
fit_fs <- iv_robust(y ~ x | z, data = dat, first_stage = TRUE)
tidy(fit_fs$first_stage$x)

# Weak-instrument, endogeneity, and overidentification tests
summary(iv_robust(y ~ x | z, data = dat, diagnostics = TRUE))

# Anderson-Rubin confidence set: the values of b at which the regression of
# y - b * x on the instrument finds no effect of z. range() reports the set
# as one interval, so check ar_p for a set with more than one piece.
b_grid <- seq(-2, 4, by = 0.01)
ar_p <- sapply(b_grid, function(b) {
  lm_robust(I(y - b * x) ~ z, data = dat)$p.value[["z"]]
})
range(b_grid[ar_p > 0.05])
confint(fit)["x", ]

}
